Yes. A boundary can be real in operation before it is real in language.

An animal does not need the word “territory” to patrol a perimeter. A cell does not need the concept “inside/outside” to maintain a membrane gradient. A nervous system does not need a verbal category before it distinguishes safe from unsafe, familiar from unfamiliar, self from environment. The discrimination can exist first as behavior, chemistry, geometry, recurrence, or response.

Naming comes later as a kind of compression layer. It takes a distributed pattern and gives it a handle. Once you have the handle, you can compare it, teach it, combine it with other handles, reason about it recursively, and move it into domains where the original pattern was never directly experienced.

So there are at least three layers:

structure -> discrimination -> articulation

The structure may exist independently. The organism or system detects a difference. Language then makes that difference portable.

And your last point is important: “order” does not have to mean a simple deterministic rule. A system can be noisy, probabilistic, chaotic, adaptive, or partially unknown and still have structure. Even randomness is defined relative to a space of possibilities and a distribution. Purely unconstrained behavior is almost impossible to distinguish from absence of a system.

The useful Pantheon move is this:

before asking “what is this called?” ask “what invariant behavior tells us a boundary exists?”

Then naming becomes a second step rather than the source of the phenomenon.

That also explains why articulation is powerful without being primary. Language does not create every distinction, but it can stabilize, transmit, recombine, and operationalize distinctions that were already being enacted.

So in a sense:

behavior discovers the boundary; language gives it a passport.